---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works — Kvendra"
  description="How the three open-core pieces of Kvendra fit together over MCP: Claude Code as the host, the self-hosted Kvendra Platform knowledge base, the Skills plugin, and the optional CLI security broker."
>
  <article class="kvd-prose">
    <span class="kvd-kicker">How it works</span>
    <h1>How the pieces fit together</h1>
    <p>
      Kvendra is three open-core pieces wired together over the Model Context
      Protocol (MCP), with one in the middle: your AI coding agent. The core is
      <strong>Kvendra Platform</strong> — a self-hosted knowledge base. Skills
      run on top of it, and an optional CLI adds a security broker. Everything
      below is something you can read, run and own.
    </p>

    <h2>The three pieces</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Kvendra Platform</strong> is the knowledge base and the only
      required piece. It runs as an MCP HTTP server on port <code>7777</code>,
      backed by PostgreSQL with the <code>pgvector</code> extension. It holds
      typed entities, runs transactions, and writes a server-side changelog, so
      the things your agent learns about a project live in a database you
      control — not in scattered notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Skills</strong> are a Claude Code plugin. They turn pipelines —
      planning a feature, running tests, cutting a release — into one-command
      workflows by calling the Platform's tools. The recipes live in the
      knowledge base, so the skills stay thin and portable.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>CLI &amp; Broker</strong> is the optional, pre-alpha security
      layer. It runs a second MCP server over stdio: a zero-knowledge vault plus
      a capability broker that performs credentialed operations on your behalf,
      so the model never sees a raw token. Skills and the Platform work without
      it; you add it only when a workflow needs brokered system operations.
    </p>

    <h2>How they are wired</h2>
    <p>
      Your agent (Claude Code, the recommended host) speaks MCP. It talks to the
      Platform to read and write the knowledge base, and — if it is installed —
      to the CLI broker to run secured operations. The diagram below traces the
      wiring.
    </p>

    <figure class="kvd-diagram" role="img"
      aria-label="Claude Code, the host, sits in the center. It connects over MCP HTTP on port 7777 to Kvendra Platform, the knowledge base on Postgres and pgvector holding typed entities, transactions and a changelog. The Skills plugin runs on top of the Platform. Claude Code also connects, optionally, over MCP stdio to the CLI broker with its zero-knowledge vault.">
      <div class="kvd-diagram__grid">
        <!-- Left rail: Platform + Skills (the KB stack) -->
        <div class="kvd-diagram__col kvd-diagram__col--left">
          <div class="kvd-diagram__node kvd-diagram__node--core">
            <span class="kvd-diagram__tag">Core · required</span>
            <strong>Kvendra Platform</strong>
            <span class="kvd-diagram__sub">MCP HTTP · :7777</span>
            <span class="kvd-diagram__sub"
              >Postgres + pgvector · typed entities · TXN · changelog</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="kvd-diagram__stack-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>runs on</span>
          </div>
          <div class="kvd-diagram__node">
            <strong>Skills</strong>
            <span class="kvd-diagram__sub"
              >Claude Code plugin · pipeline workflows</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Center: host -->
        <div class="kvd-diagram__col kvd-diagram__col--center">
          <div class="kvd-diagram__edge kvd-diagram__edge--left" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="kvd-diagram__edge-label">MCP HTTP</span>
          </div>
          <div class="kvd-diagram__node kvd-diagram__node--host">
            <strong>Claude Code</strong>
            <span class="kvd-diagram__sub">the host · speaks MCP</span>
          </div>
          <div class="kvd-diagram__edge kvd-diagram__edge--right" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="kvd-diagram__edge-label">MCP stdio · optional</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Right rail: optional CLI -->
        <div class="kvd-diagram__col kvd-diagram__col--right">
          <div class="kvd-diagram__node kvd-diagram__node--optional">
            <span class="kvd-diagram__tag kvd-diagram__tag--opt"
              >Optional · pre-alpha</span
            >
            <strong>CLI &amp; Broker</strong>
            <span class="kvd-diagram__sub">MCP stdio</span>
            <span class="kvd-diagram__sub"
              >zero-knowledge vault · capability broker</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Claude Code reads and writes the knowledge base on the Platform over MCP
        HTTP, and (when present) routes secured operations through the CLI broker
        over MCP stdio.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The flow, step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Your agent needs project context, so it queries the Platform over MCP —
        semantic search and typed lookups across the knowledge base.
      </li>
      <li>
        As work lands, the agent opens a transaction and creates or updates
        entities; the Platform records every change in a typed changelog.
      </li>
      <li>
        A skill orchestrates the larger workflow — a feature pipeline, a release
        — by chaining those same Platform tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        When a step needs a credentialed operation (push to a remote, call an
        API), and the optional CLI is installed, the agent asks the broker to
        run it. The broker resolves the secret from the vault and never hands it
        back.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Ready to wire it up? The <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Quickstart</a>
      walks through it in order — Platform first, then the rest.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Hand-drawn architecture diagram — pure CSS/HTML on the design tokens.
     No external deps, no Mermaid. Three columns on desktop; stacks on mobile.
     The grid never overflows the page: it scrolls inside its own container if
     it must, but the layout is built to fit. */
  .kvd-diagram {
    margin: var(--sp-6) 0 var(--sp-7);
  }

  .kvd-diagram__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    padding: var(--sp-5);
    background: var(--diagram-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-soft);
  }

  .kvd-diagram__col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--sp-3);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .kvd-diagram__col--center {
    align-items: center;
  }

  .kvd-diagram__node {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--sp-1);
    padding: var(--sp-4);
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--diagram-stroke);
    color: var(--diagram-fg);
  }
  .kvd-diagram__node strong {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--fs-body);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
  }
  .kvd-diagram__node--host {
    border-color: var(--phos);
    border-width: 2px;
  }
  .kvd-diagram__node--core {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--phos);
  }
  .kvd-diagram__node--optional {
    border-style: dashed;
    opacity: 0.92;
  }

  .kvd-diagram__sub {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-mono);
    color: var(--fog);
  }

  .kvd-diagram__tag {
    align-self: flex-start;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-tick);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--phos-readable);
  }
  .kvd-diagram__tag--opt {
    color: var(--fog);
  }

  /* Vertical "runs on" connector between Platform and Skills. */
  .kvd-diagram__stack-arrow {
    position: relative;
    height: var(--sp-6);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .kvd-diagram__stack-arrow::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--diagram-stroke);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }
  .kvd-diagram__stack-arrow span {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--diagram-bg);
    padding: 0 var(--sp-2);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-mono);
    color: var(--fog);
  }

  /* Horizontal connectors from the host to each rail. */
  .kvd-diagram__edge {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--diagram-stroke);
    opacity: 0.55;
    margin-block: var(--sp-3);
  }
  .kvd-diagram__edge--right {
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      to right,
      var(--diagram-stroke) 0,
      var(--diagram-stroke) 5px,
      transparent 5px,
      transparent 10px
    );
  }
  .kvd-diagram__edge-label {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    background: var(--diagram-bg);
    padding: 0 var(--sp-2);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-mono);
    color: var(--fog);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .kvd-diagram figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--sp-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--fog);
  }

  /* Mobile: stack everything into one column, top to bottom, with vertical
     connectors. No horizontal scroll. */
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .kvd-diagram__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--sp-4);
    }
    .kvd-diagram__edge {
      width: 2px;
      height: var(--sp-6);
      margin: 0 auto;
      background: var(--diagram-stroke);
    }
    .kvd-diagram__edge--right {
      background: repeating-linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        var(--diagram-stroke) 0,
        var(--diagram-stroke) 5px,
        transparent 5px,
        transparent 10px
      );
    }
    .kvd-diagram__col--center {
      order: -1;
    }
  }
</style>
